Our results by running the evaluator on the four programs:

    > open ParseAndRunHigher;;
    > 
    - run (fromString @"let add x = let f y = x+y in f end
    -                    in add 2 5 end");;
    val it: HigherFun.value = Int 7

    > run (fromString @"let add x = let f y = x+y in f end
    -                    in let addtwo = add 2
    -                    in addtwo 5 end
    -                    end");;
    val it: HigherFun.value = Int 7

    > run (fromString @"let add x = let f y = x+y in f end
    -                    in let addtwo = add 2
    -                    in let x = 77 in addtwo 5 end
    -                    end
    -                    end");;
    val it: HigherFun.value = Int 7

    > run (fromString @"let add x = let f y = x+y in f end
    -                    in add 2 end");;
    val it: HigherFun.value =
    Closure
        ("f", "y", Prim ("+", Var "x", Var "y"),
        [("x", Int 2);
        ("add",
        Closure
            ("add", "x", Letfun ("f", "y", Prim ("+", Var "x", Var "y"), Var "f"),
            []))])

Exercise 6.1 - Is the result of the third one as expected?
    The result 'Int 7' is expected. 'f' keeps x = 2 from its own declaration, so the 'let x = 77' has no effect.

Exercise 6.1 - Explain the result of the last one:
    'add 2' applies only 'add' when given the body 'let f y = x+y in f end' which evaluates to 'f' itself, therefore there is no further call.
    Hihger-order functions is supported in HigherFun.fs and therefore a function can be returned as a value.
    Here the result is a closure for 'f', carrying its declaration enviroment where x is already bound to 2.   